1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,457
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,457 --> 00:00:04,602
Episode 7.

3
00:00:04,602 --> 00:00:07,329
A Day for a Tiny Planet.

4
00:00:07,329 --> 00:00:10,170
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,170 --> 00:00:13,849
What movement mainly causes day and night on Earth?

6
00:00:13,849 --> 00:00:15,340
Ready?

7
00:00:15,340 --> 00:00:17,514
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,514 --> 00:00:21,079
Leo draws a picture of the Sun going to bed.

9
00:00:21,079 --> 00:00:24,744
It wears pajamas and carries a toothbrush.

10
00:00:24,744 --> 00:00:27,343
Is that why it gets dark?

11
00:00:27,343 --> 00:00:29,204
Pip asks.

12
00:00:29,204 --> 00:00:32,798
In my picture, yes, says Leo.

13
00:00:32,798 --> 00:00:35,951
In real life, probably not.

14
00:00:35,951 --> 00:00:39,318
Ms. Park puts a globe beside a lamp.

15
00:00:39,318 --> 00:00:42,102
The lamp represents the Sun.

16
00:00:42,102 --> 00:00:46,335
She places a small sticker on the globe for their school.

17
00:00:46,335 --> 00:00:49,857
At first, the sticker faces the lamp.

18
00:00:49,857 --> 00:00:53,963
This side is receiving sunlight, Maya says.

19
00:00:53,963 --> 00:00:56,875
Then Ms. Park turns the globe.

20
00:00:56,875 --> 00:00:59,957
The sticker moves into the dark side.

21
00:00:59,957 --> 00:01:02,258
Did the lamp turn off?

22
00:01:02,258 --> 00:01:04,161
she asks.

23
00:01:04,161 --> 00:01:05,710
No.

24
00:01:05,710 --> 00:01:09,531
Our part of the globe turned away, says Leo.

25
00:01:09,531 --> 00:01:12,642
They slowly rotate the globe again.

26
00:01:12,642 --> 00:01:15,823
Their school moves back into the light.

27
00:01:15,823 --> 00:01:19,588
Good morning, tiny school, says Pip.

28
00:01:19,588 --> 00:01:23,380
I hope your tiny students remembered their homework.

29
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:27,542
Maya adds another sticker on a distant part of the globe.

30
00:01:27,542 --> 00:01:31,946
When our sticker is in the light, that one can be in the dark.

31
00:01:31,946 --> 00:01:37,102
So people do not all eat breakfast at the same moment, Leo says.

32
00:01:37,102 --> 00:01:39,034
Pip is worried.

33
00:01:39,034 --> 00:01:41,946
How do I know when to announce lunch?

34
00:01:41,946 --> 00:01:45,440
Use the clock where you are, says Maya.

35
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:48,096
Leo changes his drawing.

36
00:01:48,096 --> 00:01:52,713
Now the Sun stays in place while a little Earth turns beside it.

37
00:01:52,713 --> 00:01:55,923
He keeps the pajamas in one corner.

38
00:01:55,923 --> 00:01:59,190
Those are for me, he explains.

39
00:01:59,190 --> 00:02:02,485
Learning about night has made me sleepy.

40
00:02:02,485 --> 00:02:05,525
Now, a little more to discover.

41
00:02:05,525 --> 00:02:07,642
Earth rotates.

42
00:02:07,642 --> 00:02:15,156
A full turn relative to the Sun takes about twenty-four hours, giving us the familiar day-and-night cycle.

43
00:02:15,156 --> 00:02:20,497
The side facing the Sun has daylight; the side facing away has night.

44
00:02:20,497 --> 00:02:24,602
Earth's yearly trip around the Sun is a different movement.

45
00:02:24,602 --> 00:02:27,571
Let us hear the main idea again.

46
00:02:27,571 --> 00:02:31,647
A lamp and a globe helped the club model day and night.

47
00:02:31,647 --> 00:02:36,789
Their school sticker moved into light and then darkness as the globe turned.

48
00:02:36,789 --> 00:02:40,482
The Sun did not need to switch off or go to bed.

49
00:02:40,482 --> 00:02:43,593
Here are three useful expressions.

50
00:02:43,593 --> 00:02:46,860
You can listen, or say them with me.

51
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:49,446
What time is it where you are?

52
00:02:49,446 --> 00:02:52,031
What time is it where you are?

53
00:02:52,031 --> 00:02:54,247
It is daytime here.

54
00:02:54,247 --> 00:02:56,463
It is daytime here.

55
00:02:56,463 --> 00:02:59,332
Our part of Earth is turning away.

56
00:02:59,332 --> 00:03:02,201
Our part of Earth is turning away.

57
00:03:02,201 --> 00:03:04,914
Remember our listening question?

58
00:03:04,914 --> 00:03:08,593
What movement mainly causes day and night on Earth?

59
00:03:08,593 --> 00:03:10,823
Here is the answer.

60
00:03:10,823 --> 00:03:16,974
Earth's rotation causes different parts of its surface to face toward or away from the Sun.

61
00:03:16,974 --> 00:03:20,795
And here is a question just for your imagination.

62
00:03:20,795 --> 00:03:26,221
If you could call a friend on the other side of Earth, what might that friend be doing?

63
00:03:26,221 --> 00:03:29,474
There is more than one possible answer.

64
00:03:29,474 --> 00:03:32,869
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
